// TikTok-Mode-only "greatly improved" explanations — natural, step-by-step,
// spoken-friendly narration written to be read aloud by TikTok Mode's TTS
// voice (see ui/tiktokMode.js), layered on top of — never replacing — the
// real question banks used by lessons/quizzes/boss quizzes elsewhere in
// the app. Every entry here carries the exact same math as the base
// bank's own `explain` field (js/data/questions/<file>.js); this only
// changes how it's explained out loud, never the answer or the numbers.
//
// Keyed by skill id -> a composite key built from the question itself,
// NOT array index (index-keying breaks silently if a bank is ever
// reordered/edited — see explainKey() in tiktokMode.js for the full
// rationale). The composite is built in three layers, each added only
// when it exists on the question:
//   1. A passage/stimulus disambiguator, when present — passageId or
//      stimulusId (ACT Reading/Science, many questions share one
//      passage/stimulus and reuse a generic stem across all of them) or
//      the full inline `passage` text (SAT/PSAT R&W, one passage per
//      question with no id, and literally the same stem reused for
//      EVERY question in the skill).
//   2. The question's own `q` text.
//   3. The question's `choices`, joined — added ALWAYS, for every
//      skill, because some skills (found first in ma-numbersense) reuse
//      a fully generic stem like "Which of the following numbers is
//      prime?" across multiple, otherwise-unrelated questions whose only
//      distinguishing content lives in the answer choices, not the stem
//      or any passage.
// A key that can't be built (missing/changed question) just falls
// through to the base bank's own `explain`, exactly like a skill with
// no overlay at all. Before adding entries for a new skill, verify this
// composite key has no duplicates within that skill's bank — collisions
// are now rare (three layers of disambiguation) but not structurally
// impossible if a skill reuses both stem AND choices.
